---
// THE TRIFECTA DECK (founder 2026-08-07, built out 2026-08-08) — one page for
// Andrea Pomarico (Four Winds Wholeness Sanctuary) and Steve Dobo (The
// Audacity Lab), who asked to see this as a single proposition rather than
// three separate conversations.
//
// SOURCES: the Gaia brief "Four Winds: The Next Phase" (July 2026, Andrea +
// Galyn) and the Four Winds acquisition proposal v2. Property facts, the
// stewardship history and Steve's role as former Four Winds board president
// all come from those documents.
//
// ⚠ DELIBERATELY CARRIES NO FINANCIAL CLAIM. Lichen is a 501(c)(3);
// "investment" means something specific and legally distinct, and the founder
// is settling the framing in their own prep document. Withheld on purpose,
// and present in the source material: the seller expectation, the term of the
// stewardship agreement, the instruments in the community-capital raise, and
// anything resembling a return. The structure below is described
// QUALITATIVELY only — roles, not terms. Do not add numbers here without the
// founder's explicit go-ahead.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import WeavePitch from '../../components/WeavePitch.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Four Winds · Audacity Lab · Lichen"
  description="Three organisations, one proposition: land held in trust, young people growing up alongside it, and the infrastructure that makes both repeatable."
>
  <WeavePitch partner="Four Winds & Audacity Lab">
    <Fragment slot="weave">
      <span class="eyebrow">Three organisations, one proposition</span>
      <h2 class="display h2-weave">What weaving in looks like</h2>

      <p class="wbody">
        Andrea, Steve — you&rsquo;ve each been having a version of this
        conversation with us separately. This page puts it in one place, because
        the three pieces only work as one thing.
      </p>

      <p class="wbody">
        Four Winds has been consciously stewarded for 144 years — homesteaded by
        Martha Bengtsson Wolfensberger around 1882, and cared for since 2011 by
        Andrea, alongside the animals, the land and the healing lineage that
        call it home. Andrea&rsquo;s stewardship cycle is complete and she is
        consciously assisting the handoff. What we&rsquo;re proposing
        doesn&rsquo;t have a name in the current system: not a single buyer, but
        a network.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">What each of us brings</h3>

      <ol class="wpaths">
        <li>
          <strong>Four Winds — the land.</strong> Thirty-five acres against the
          Front Range, between Pikes Peak and Longs Peak. The 1899 Wolfensberger
          barn, whose acoustics are said to rival Carnegie Hall. Pastures,
          ponds, ancient pines and dark skies. Horses, llamas, goats and a wild
          mustang, alongside the deer, elk, fox and owls that were already here.
          A regenerative farm with agricultural status, an organic garden and
          greenhouse, lodging and practitioner housing, and fifteen years of
          programming for veterans, first responders, trauma survivors, people
          with special needs, and anyone else who came to grow. It is also
          KAHUBA&rsquo;s headquarters — seventeen years of sanctuary work, 45+
          rescued horses, llamas, goats, dogs and feral cats given a forever
          home, and long-standing partnerships with Mt. Carmel Veterans Center,
          Wild Hearts Haven, The Imagine Project, H.O.O.V.E.S. and Purple Lotus
          WARRIOR Yoga. This becomes the first property held by Lichen&rsquo;s
          Land Trust.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Audacity Lab — the young people.</strong> Steve&rsquo;s work
          brings dreamers together to prototype the future one experiment at a
          time — Montessori microschools, the mobile classroom, and reach into
          communities that conscious-media platforms never touch. Steve is also
          a former Four Winds board president, which means he isn&rsquo;t
          arriving with a hypothesis about this land. He already knows what it
          can hold. Audacity Lab becomes the education and next-generation arm
          on the ground: young people growing up alongside land, animals and a
          working farm, learning stewardship and reciprocity by living them
          rather than reading about them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lichen — the infrastructure.</strong> The legal, business and
          technology layer that makes the other two durable. The Land Trust that
          receives what&rsquo;s given and holds the ground. The Current-cy
          economy that records every contribution once it&rsquo;s stewarded —
          hay, work-trade, animal care, the hours a teenager puts into the
          garden, the people who simply show up. And the network every
          contributor is woven into, rather than left holding a receipt.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="wbody">
        Separately, each of these is a good project that depends on someone
        staying generous. Together they&rsquo;re a structure: the land
        can&rsquo;t be sold out from under the programming, the programming has
        somewhere permanent to stand, and the contributions that keep both alive
        are counted rather than forgotten.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">How each of you shows up on the platform</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        Lichen holds one person and a whole valley on the same terms. Here is
        what that means concretely for the two of you — not a metaphor, but the
        actual objects in the system, live today.
      </p>

      <ul class="wexamples">
        <li>
          <strong>Four Winds becomes a place.</strong> Every room and thing it
          stewards goes onto a real calendar — the barn, the arena, the therapy
          rooms, the guest house, the stables, the truck, the long tables. What
          the sanctuary already has stops sitting idle, and anyone booking it
          does so through a steward who approves each request. The land itself
          is a member, not a backdrop: a beyond-human entity whose contributions
          are recorded, and whose care is funded.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Audacity Lab becomes an organization.</strong> Its Lichen page
          is a real website on the open web — programmes, offerings, a calendar,
          bookable sessions, and a team — updating itself as the work changes,
          because it draws from the same listings members already see. Donors,
          schools and families find it without a separate site to maintain.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Audacity Lab also becomes a community.</strong> The
          organization is the public face; the community is the belonging.
          Families, educators, alumni and volunteers join through request and
          approval, with groups nested inside it — a cohort, a build team, a
          board — each with its own chat, calendar, events and find-a-time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The young people become members.</strong> Not audience,
          members: with their own contributions recorded in an economy that
          counts an afternoon of fence-mending and an hour of teaching as the
          real things they are. A young person who grows up inside this leaves
          with a ledger of what they gave, and a web of people who know it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Each programme becomes a group.</strong> A season, a cohort, a
          research visit — its own room, its own calendar, graduating on its own
          timeline while the community underneath it persists.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>KAHUBA keeps its own front door.</strong> Andrea&rsquo;s
          nonprofit is its own organization on the platform, with its own page,
          its own donors and its own programmes — and the partner nonprofits it
          has worked beside for years can each hold their own, booking the
          sanctuary&rsquo;s rooms and land through the same calendar rather than
          through a phone tree. The co-op model KAHUBA has run on goodwill for
          seventeen years finally gets the machinery it deserved.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">What Four Winds proves</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        Four Winds is uniquely ready to be first — healed, turnkey, legally and
        agriculturally established. It is the pilot: the place where Lichen&rsquo;s
        model stops being an argument and becomes an address people can visit,
        measure and belong to. Audacity Lab is a partner in that pilot, not a
        tenant of it. What the education arm learns here — how young people
        change when they grow up alongside land held in common — is part of what
        the pilot is for, and it travels to every property that follows.
      </p>

      <p class="wcallout">
        The land can&rsquo;t be sold out from under the work. That is the whole
        point, and it is the one promise no ordinary sale can make.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">The economy is already running there</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        Walking through the operating history, the striking thing isn&rsquo;t
        the costs. It&rsquo;s how much of Four Winds already runs on exactly
        what Lichen was built to count. Land care happens through work-trade. A
        neighbour farms the fields in exchange for half the crop. The vet
        donates her services because she loves what the education centre does.
        Students get paid to stack hay. Llama fibre and pellets hold the
        agricultural status. Volunteers arrive, and some of them stay.
      </p>

      <p class="wbody">
        None of that appears in any ledger. It is a reciprocity economy already
        operating in the analog — held together by Andrea&rsquo;s relationships
        and fifteen years of goodwill, and invisible the moment she steps back.
        The gap isn&rsquo;t generosity; Four Winds has never lacked that. The
        gap is that nobody has ever <em>counted</em> it, which means it
        can&rsquo;t be seen by a funder, scaled to another property, or handed
        to a successor. That is precisely the gap Current-cy closes: every hour,
        every bale, every trade recorded and returned to the person who gave it.
      </p>

      <p class="wbody">
        And what the land actually needs turns out to be smaller and more
        concrete than the conversation usually assumes: a resourced steward.
        Someone living there whose job is the place.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">Somewhere to start</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        The geothermal greenhouse is the right first project — modest, in the
        ground before winter, mentors already willing, and useful the day it
        opens. It grows food, it demonstrates the sustainability argument
        rather than asserting it, and it is exactly the kind of thing a
        fifteen-year-old can help build and then point at for the rest of their
        life. Small enough to finish; real enough to prove the pattern.
      </p>

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">The shape of it</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        Roles, not terms — the numbers belong in a conversation with counsel,
        not on a web page:
      </p>

      <ol class="wpaths">
        <li>
          <strong>Title is held in permanent trust</strong> by a purpose-formed
          nonprofit — never resold, subdivided or cashed out.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Acquisition is funded by a community</strong>, not a single
          check, so the land stays beholden to no one owner and the structure
          embodies the thesis instead of contradicting it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Lichen stewards and operates</strong> under a long-term
          agreement — land, water, animals, ecosystem, maintenance, insurance
          and programming — with its capacity underwritten rather than left to
          carry thirty-five acres unsupported.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Andrea&rsquo;s conditions become recorded covenants</strong> —
          safety and clarity of the land, lifetime care for the sanctuary
          animals, no extractive development, continuity of healing purpose —
          surviving any future transfer. That is what makes a handoff
          trustworthy to a steward of fifteen years.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Audacity Lab wraps in as the education arm</strong>, with a
          permanent stake in the classroom rather than visiting rights to a
          site — and, if it suits Steve, potentially as the umbrella under
          which the property&rsquo;s own nonprofit component is formed. That
          was raised on the fourth and is worth deciding on purpose rather than
          by default.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="wbody">
        One more thing worth saying out loud, because it came up and it
        matters: this is philanthropy that gives something back. Not a tax
        receipt and a newsletter — a place you can visit, a ledger showing
        exactly what your giving moved, and a community you belong to rather
        than fund from a distance. Founders would be a real community on the
        platform and in person, with an annual gathering on the land. Giving
        that reconnects the giver is a different instrument from giving that
        discharges an obligation, and it is the one Lichen is built for.
      </p>

      <!-- ── THE ASK ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
           Still deliberately empty. The founder is settling the structure of
           what's being offered — philanthropic support for Lichen, a 501(c)(3),
           versus participation in the Four Winds land trust; different
           instruments, different legal footing — in their own prep document,
           which this session could not open. Nothing here states or implies a
           number, a term or a return until that arrives. -->

      <h3 class="display h-2 wsub">What we&rsquo;re asking, for now</h3>

      <p class="wbody">
        Nothing with a number on it. The financial structure is being settled
        properly, with counsel, and you&rsquo;ll see it written down before
        you&rsquo;re asked to react to it. What we&rsquo;re asking now is
        smaller and more useful: that the three of us walk the land together, in
        the same place at the same time, and work out the shape of it there.
      </p>

      <p class="wbody">
        Read the full story — the ecosystem, the trust model, the economy and
        the five ways to weave in — on the <a href="/about" class="wlink">About
        page</a>. This page is only about what the three of us could build
        together.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </WeavePitch>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .h2-weave { font-size: clamp(24px, 3.5vw, 36px); margin: 4px 0 0; }
</style>
